Now let's set up the noise analogy. The banana man is the noise. One jarring sound. The random clothes are generic music (not a very nice term okay but it is the sort of music that you wouldn't be able to say much about except that it was 'nice'. IF on the other hand you are a tailor, of course you would be more equipped to say something about the other people's clothes. If you are a fashion historian, too, you would be able to perhaps say more about the everyday clothes than the banana man. The clothes might even interest you more than our banana man. Of course if you are really into bananas you might have a lot of relevant things to say about the banana man but that would be like being a pipe specialist and listening to something with the sound pod a clanging pipe and being able to say what material and diameter the pipe sampled is (that I suppose is doable). 

If we increase the number of banana men it won't necessarily make the experience more memorable, but it would construct a situation instead of a fact about the banana man. 

NOW if we introduced a woman dressed up as a doctor with a stethoscope this is where it gets interesting. We see a banana, we see a doctor with a stethoscope. The other people in the crowd become more like us, looking at the banana and the doctor. Even if they are all looking away. There is a unity created between the two costumed people. There is a narrative that becomes apparent. There is a relationship. There is a dynamic. We see them in relation of each other. 

We take Aqui Alli Gatto... it's a track. It starts with some sort of radio talking, under meowing cats, soon followed by vocal shouts that repeat until the guitar comes in. WHY is there a radio, WHY are there cats, WHY and WHAT are the vocals doing?

This is what I like about industrial music. The opening up beyond the realm of musical instruments. There always needs to be a why. Sure chords have a WHY. But they have. MUSICAL WHY, not a REALLY WHY. 

There is a superfluousness about the bananaman that makes him so interesting. We know that he is likely not doing this to be comfortable... and if he is that is an even bigger WHY. The doctor, on the other hand raises other questions entirely. This is the distinction we can draw about different kinds of 'noise' in music. 


